About this lecture
Almost everything you will learn in this course about where things are in a cell was first seen in a microscope. Today is about how those pictures are made, and how far you can trust them.
After this lecture you can:

explain the difference between magnification and resolution, and why a light microscope cannot separate two objects closer than about 200 nm;
explain how phase contrast, DIC and fluorescence make transparent cells visible;
describe how we label one kind of protein: immunofluorescence (fixed cells) and fluorescent proteins such as GFP (living cells);
say what confocal, light-sheet, super-resolution and electron microscopy (TEM, SEM, cryo-EM, tomography) add;
choose a method for a question, and read a microscopy figure critically.

ELO 1 (cell and methods) and ELO 6 (methods to study the cytoskeleton).

This lecture follows the tour of the cell. In the tour we showed you organelles in pictures; today we ask how those pictures were made. Every microscope answers three questions in its own way. First, can it make small things big enough and sharp enough to separate them? That is resolution. Second, can it make a transparent cell stand out from the water around it? That is contrast. Third, can it show one particular molecule among thousands? That is specificity, or labelling. Keep these three words in mind; every method today improves one or more of them, and every method pays a price, usually that the cell must be dead, or that the picture is blurrier, or slower. A thread runs through the whole lecture: “How do we know?” A picture is evidence, and you need to know what a method can and cannot show before you believe it. This covers ELO 1, choosing a method, and supports ELO 6, because many of the examples are the cytoskeleton.

About this lecture
Every compartment in the cell is bounded by a membrane. Today: what a membrane is made of, why it forms by itself, why it is liquid, and how proteins sit in it. This is the base for the next lectures on transporters, pumps and ion channels.
After this lecture you can:

explain why lipids self-assemble into a bilayer (the hydrophobic effect), and name the main lipid classes;
explain what sets membrane fluidity (tail length, double bonds, cholesterol, temperature);
explain lipid asymmetry, and what flippases and scramblases do;
describe how membrane proteins are attached, and predict transmembrane helices from a hydropathy plot;
explain how FRAP, cell fusion and freeze-fracture EM showed that the membrane is a fluid with proteins in it;
explain how lipid nanoparticles and liposomes use these rules to deliver drugs.

ELO 2 (membranes and transport).

This is the first lecture of the membranes block. You met membranes in biochemistry: phospholipids, the bilayer, amphipathic molecules. We will not repeat that course. Instead we ask the cell biologist’s questions. Why does a bilayer form at all, and why does it seal itself? Why is it liquid, and how does a cell keep it at the right fluidity? Why are the two halves of the bilayer different, and what happens when that difference is lost? How do proteins sit in a membrane, and how can you predict that from a sequence? And how do we know any of this? At the end we look at medicines that are built from the same rules: the lipid nanoparticles in mRNA vaccines, and liposomes that carry a cancer drug. Everything today belongs to ELO 2. The next two lectures, on transport and on ion channels, use the membrane proteins we meet today.

About this lecture
Every cell has a voltage across its plasma membrane. Nerves and muscles use it to send signals, but so does every other cell: to move salt and water, to take up nutrients and to trigger secretion. Today: the proteins that make and use this voltage, the ion channels.
After this lecture you can:

explain how a channel differs from a transporter (rate, selectivity, gating);
explain why the K⁺ channel lets K⁺ through but not the smaller Na⁺;
describe the three main ways a channel opens: voltage, ligand, mechanical force;
derive the Nernst equation from ΔG and use it to calculate an equilibrium potential;
explain how the resting membrane potential arises, and what happens in an action potential;
explain how patch clamp records one channel, and how channel defects and channel drugs act (cystic fibrosis, local anaesthetics).

ELO 2 (membranes and transport), with a disease example for ELO 9.

This lecture builds on the two lectures before it. In the lecture on membrane structure you saw that a lipid bilayer is almost impermeable to ions. In the lecture on transporters and pumps you saw how the sodium-potassium pump uses ATP to build concentration gradients: high potassium inside, high sodium outside. Today we ask what happens when we open a hole for one kind of ion. The answer is a voltage, the membrane potential, and that voltage is used by every cell, not only nerves. We will use your chemistry: the Gibbs energy, delta G, which you know from equilibria, gives us the Nernst equation in three lines. We also meet one of the great methods of cell biology, patch clamp, which lets you watch a single protein molecule open and close in real time. We finish with two medical stories: cystic fibrosis, where modern drugs are matched to the exact molecular defect, and local anaesthetics, which you have probably met at the dentist. This covers ELO 2 and gives an example for ELO 9.

About this lecture
A lipid bilayer keeps almost everything out. Yet a cell must take up sugars, amino acids and ions, throw out waste and drugs, and keep the inside very different from the outside. Today: the proteins that move molecules across membranes, and where the energy comes from.
After this lecture you can:

predict which molecules cross a pure lipid bilayer, and explain why most others need a protein;
explain passive and active transport with ΔG, and why transporters saturate like enzymes;
describe the alternating-access mechanism and uniport, symport and antiport;
explain primary active transport (P-type, V-type and ABC pumps; the Na⁺/K⁺ pump) and secondary active transport driven by the Na⁺ gradient;
explain how an epithelium moves glucose from the gut to the blood, and how drugs act on transporters.

ELO 2 (membranes and transport).

In the lecture on membrane structure you met the lipid bilayer: a thin, oily sheet that separates two water-filled spaces. Today we ask how anything gets through it. The answer has three parts. First, physics: a few small, uncharged molecules simply dissolve in the bilayer and cross on their own, but most of what a cell needs cannot. Second, proteins: transporters and channels give polar molecules and ions a path. Third, energy: to move something against its concentration gradient, the cell must pay, either directly with ATP or indirectly with another gradient. You already know the tools from chemistry and biochemistry: ΔG tells us which way a process goes, and Michaelis–Menten kinetics describes a protein with a limited number of binding sites. We will use both. Channels and the membrane potential get their own lecture next, so today channels appear only as a contrast to transporters. This lecture covers ELO 2, and it ends with drugs that act on transporters, which you meet again in drug development.
